WEBVTT
Language: en

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.440
Memory management by the operating system.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:10.900
The goal of this video is to understand
how an operating system frees and allocates

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.900
memory space to the different programs.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.060
To do this, we need to understand
that there are two types of memory:

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.480
main memory and secondary memory.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.600
Main memory is made up of RAM and ROM.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.040
RAM stores the information a program needs

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.100
while it runs.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.120
It accesses data regardless of
the order in which it is stored.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.240
This memory loses its data
when it stops receiving power.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.020
ROM is read-only;

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.040
it stores the essential
software the computer needs

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.800
to boot. It checks that the
components are working correctly,

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.040
and it keeps its information
whether it receives power

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.010
or not. Secondary memory,
on the other hand, is where

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.610
programs and files are stored permanently.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:57.090
For example, a solid-state
drive or a hard disk drive.

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:59.010
When a program runs,

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.430
its files are sent to main memory.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:06.590
The Memory Management Unit tells
main memory the position on disk

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:07.910
of the program's data.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.770
Main memory retrieves the data
from the physical addresses,

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.650
which is where the data is
located on the hard drive,

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:17.730
creating a base address, which is
where the data will be located in main

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:21.670
memory. Each byte on the hard
drive has its own physical address.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:23.230
Once in main memory,

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:27.430
data belonging to the same process is
stored according to its base register,

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:31.310
which serves as a reference to locate
a particular address, and the limit

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:34.650
register, which helps determine
the offset within an address.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:38.270
There are different ways to
allocate process space in main

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.850
memory. The main ones
are static partitions.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.960
Memory is divided so that each
partition holds one process.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.920
Partitions can each be a different size.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:52.940
The operating system creates a table of
partitions and processes, indicating whether

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:54.660
the partition is occupied or free.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.500
And which process occupies the partition?

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.580
Dynamic partitions.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.460
The operating system places
processes consecutively in memory,

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.200
making more efficient use of memory.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.400
The operating system creates a
process table with the identifier,

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.320
the base address, the size,

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.500
the process state,
the control block address;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.840
likewise, it creates a table of the
free memory fragments, indicating

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.880
their base address and size.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.680
When data is added or grows,

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.080
the tables are updated.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.780
Memory management by segmentation

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.660
In this approach, memory is divided
according to the existing segments.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.380
Processes are divided
into different segments.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.440
There are code segments,
stack segments, and data segments.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.320
Each segment is smaller than a partition,

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.240
because if a partition held a process,

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.100
that partition could be
divided into different,

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:50.800
smaller segments, which can be freed
without affecting the whole process.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.940
This makes freeing memory easier,

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.980
because when a process
isn't using a segment,

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.200
it can be removed from memory

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.400
to make room for another waiting segment.
